Suppose that ⟦0⟧ is closed;
  we will show that so too is ⟦1⟧.
  As we already know that ⟦2⟧ is open, thus locally constructible, it remains only to show that it is stable under specialisation, which comes from the fact that it is a union of closed subsets, namely inverse images under the ⟦3⟧-th power homomorphisms ⟦4⟧ of the closed subset ⟦5⟧.

This means that, for every integer ⟦0⟧, the set of ⟦1⟧ such that ⟦2⟧ is locally constructible;
      but this is the difference between ⟦3⟧ and the union of the ⟦4⟧ where ⟦5⟧ runs over the proper divisors of ⟦6⟧;
      since ⟦7⟧ is locally constructible, so too are all the ⟦8⟧, and thus also the aforementioned difference.

Suppose that the residual characteristics of ⟦0⟧ are all ⟦1⟧, so that ⟦2⟧ and ⟦3⟧.
    Then ⟦4⟧ is open, and even open and closed if ⟦5⟧ is separated over ⟦6⟧ and the ⟦7⟧ are proper;
    under these same hypotheses, ⟦8⟧ is proper over ⟦9⟧ and thus closed in ⟦10⟧.
    Suppose finally that, for every integer ⟦11⟧, the ⟦12⟧-th power homomorphism in ⟦13⟧ is universally open, then ⟦14⟧ is open;
    if furthermore the ⟦15⟧ do not have any additive component (for example the ⟦16⟧ are proper, as above), then ⟦17⟧ is universally open, and even simple if ⟦18⟧ is reduced.

Let ⟦0⟧ be a proper and normal morphism (i.e. flat with normal geometric fibres), and suppose that ⟦1⟧ exists.
    Then ⟦2⟧ is proper over ⟦3⟧, thus closed in ⟦4⟧;
    furthermore, ⟦5⟧ and ⟦6⟧ are both closed, as is ⟦7⟧ in "equal characteristic".

In all known cases, ⟦0⟧ is universally open over ⟦1⟧, but we should probably not have excessive illusions, even if ⟦2⟧ is simple;
    in any case, Mumford has constructed an example (it is true with ⟦3⟧ non-reduced, in fact with ⟦4⟧ the spectrum of an Artinian ring) where ⟦5⟧ is not flat over ⟦6⟧, by infinitesimally varying the Igusa surface.
    The point envisaged by Mumford can also be found in ⟦7⟧, and it remains possible (for ⟦8⟧ simple) that ⟦9⟧ is flat over ⟦10⟧ at the points of ⟦11⟧;
    the speaker doubts, however, that this is always the case, even when restricting to the points of ⟦12⟧ and supposing ⟦13⟧ to be the spectrum of a discrete valuation ring.
    The question is linked to the study of fixed points of an abelian scheme under a finite automorphism group, a situation for which we seem to lack examples.
    It seems that even by restricting to simple and projective ⟦14⟧, the results of local regularity on ⟦15⟧ stated in the present section, and the conjectures raised in ⟦16⟧, basically exhaust what can be said on this subject without more particular hypotheses on the nature of the fibres of ⟦17⟧.
    We recall, however, that, if the geometric fibres of ⟦18⟧ are ⟦19⟧ and have no additive component, then it follows from ⟦20⟧ and ⟦21⟧ that ⟦22⟧ is simple over ⟦23⟧ at the points of ⟦24⟧ whenever ⟦25⟧ is reduced;
    this result still holds even, if ⟦26⟧ is normal, without any hypothesis on ⟦27⟧, and we will see in ⟦28⟧.
    On this note, we point out:

Claim (i) follows from ⟦0⟧ and ⟦1⟧, and (ii) follows from the infinitesimal criterion for simple morphisms and a well-known obstruction calculation, taking into account the fact that (by the "semi-continuous theorem") the hypothesis made on ⟦2⟧ will still hold true for neighbouring points.
      Finally, (iii) follows from the fact that ⟦3⟧ is isomorphic to the Zariski tangent space at the identity element of ⟦4⟧;
      the last claim is a particular case of a theorem of Cartier, saying that a "formal group" in characteristic ⟦5⟧ is formally simple over ⟦6⟧.
